  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nOn Wednesday and Thursday, the upper-level pattern starts to\ntransition into zonal flow as the system from Tuesday finally\nexits out of the area as a trough to the west begins to move\neast and pass over the area midday Thursday. Then on Friday and\nthe remainder of the period, we can expect some slight ridging\nbefore turning back to a broad and slow moving trough that could\ndig into the County Warning Area (CWA). While confidence for the\nevolution for the overall pattern on Thursday increases from low \nto medium (30% to 50%), there is still some uncertainty with the\noverall pattern on Thursday as a possible cut-off low from the\nsystem on Tuesday could form just north of the area and combine\nwith the primary trough. For the rest of the period, ensemble\nspread does show some variability of the ridge and could amplify\nto the north on Friday. This could then potentially push the\nbroad trough on Saturday to the southwest and stall over the\nNevada and Utah border. There is low confidence (25%) that the \nridge would amplify to the north.\n\nTemperatures throughout second half of next week look to be \nfairly consistent. While afternoon highs are forecast to stay in\nthe 50s, the morning hours will likely be a little cold as\ntemperatures over the Tri-State border continue to be at or\nnearly above freezing with slightly warmer temperatures towards\nthe east. \n\nFor precipitation, a brief shower could happen Thursday as a\nweak cold front passes through with the trough. While there is\nsome vorticity over the area, the main limiting factor is the\nlack of mid-layer moisture aloft.However, there is low \nconfidence (30%) that showers could occur. If showers do occur\nit is favored to be south of I-70 within the very early morning\nhours. On Saturday, there could be a chance for showers\nthroughout the day as the broad trough passes through. However\nwith the low confidence within the patterns evolution, chances\nfor showers could diminish as time progresses closer. On late\nSaturday night, with the freezing temperatures and the\npossibility for precipitation, there is a potential for\nrain/snow mix within far western Kit Carson County. Although, no\nsnow accumulation is expected at this point.",
